# Who must review what. GitHub requests these reviewers automatically.
#
# Every rule names both of us, and that is deliberate rather than lazy.
# GitHub never counts a pull request author's own approval, so a rule naming
# one person makes that person's own changes to that path unmergeable by
# anyone, admins included. The pin bump mandated by README.md is the clearest
# case: only the supervisor will ever author one, so naming only the
# supervisor would make every pin bump a dead end.
#
# CODEOWNERS also cannot express "both must approve" — two names on a line
# means any one of them satisfies the rule. Who reviews what is therefore a
# convention, written below and in README.md, not a mechanism:
#
#   the dialect, the passes and the tool   the student writes, the supervisor reviews
#   tests, runtime, benchmarks, CI         the supervisor writes, the student reviews
#   decisions and docs                     whoever did not write it
#
# The point of the split is that whoever does not own a part is the one who
# reads it. That only works if both of us keep to it; nothing here enforces it.

*                       @apaznikov @ZweZeya

/mlir/                  @apaznikov @ZweZeya
/shim/                  @apaznikov @ZweZeya
/test/                  @apaznikov @ZweZeya
/runtime/               @apaznikov @ZweZeya
/benchmarks/            @apaznikov @ZweZeya
/docker/                @apaznikov @ZweZeya
/.github/               @apaznikov @ZweZeya
/decisions/             @apaznikov @ZweZeya
/docs/                  @apaznikov @ZweZeya

# The pinned LLVM revision still moves only by its own deliberate pull
# request, per decisions/0001. That is a rule about how, not about who.
/llvm                   @apaznikov @ZweZeya
/.gitmodules            @apaznikov @ZweZeya
